--TEST--
`Core\Html::join` writes its parts in order with the separator between each pair and nowhere else, and the answer is a `Markup` — `rule:core-classes/html-template`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The list form of `Markup + Markup`: a page composed from fragments writes
// this instead of folding the operator, and the separator is what the operator
// has no room for.
array<Core\Html\Markup> $rows = [
    "<li>one</li>" as Core\Html\Markup,
    "<li>two</li>" as Core\Html\Markup,
    "<li>three</li>" as Core\Html\Markup,
];
echo Core\Html::join($rows, "<br>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// Between each *pair*: never ahead of the first part and never after the last,
// which a single part is what shows — one part has no pair, so it is written
// alone however loud the separator is.
array<Core\Html\Markup> $one = ["<li>only</li>" as Core\Html\Markup];
echo Core\Html::join($one, "<br>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// The answer is a `Markup` and not merely bytes that print the same: the
// declared type asserts it, and joining the result again is what would stop
// compiling if `join` answered a `string`.
Core\Html\Markup $list = Core\Html::join($rows, "" as Core\Html\Markup);
array<Core\Html\Markup> $twice = [$list, $list];
echo Core\Html::join($twice, "<hr>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";

// A `Markup` is a value type, so composing leaves every operand where it was.
echo Core\Html::join($one, "<br>" as Core\Html\Markup), "\n";
--EXPECT--
<li>one</li><br><li>two</li><br><li>three</li>
<li>only</li>
<li>one</li><li>two</li><li>three</li><hr><li>one</li><li>two</li><li>three</li>
<li>only</li>
